From e9be976d730af751f8d4adede0da2a73a150bea3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MarsTechHAN <hansh-sz@hotmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 22:51:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] [CuTe, SM80] Dense-order block walk (bit-exact vs dense)
 + DenseBlockMaskTorch input

- Walk the partial and full lists as ONE merged sequence in the dense loop's
  order (descending KV blocks in forward, ascending Q blocks in backward),
  with a per-block partial flag checked inside the mask function instead of
  two separate loops. A sparse run is now bit-identical to the dense kernel
  with an equivalent mask_mod (forward O/LSE; backward dK/dV -- dQ uses fp32
  atomics). Also halves the unrolled loop bodies.
- New DenseBlockMaskTorch(block_mask [B|1, H|1, M, N] bool/uint8 with
  0 skip / 1 full / 2 partial, optional partial_kv_blocks [N], block_size)
  accepted as block_sparse_tensors on SM8x. Each CTA turns its row (forward)
  or column (backward) into a 512 B smem bitmask (ballot) and walks set
  bits, so no index lists and no transposed Q-direction lists are needed;
  FlashAttnFunc reuses the same mask for backward. Up to 2048 blocks/side.
